./LOGBOOK.md:298:- FINDING: the fixture/probe agreement check does not need a Go toolchain. `tools/toolchain_gate.py` parses the landed probe's own `goCases`/`tcCases` literals — 29 cases — and holds every fixture row marked `probe_measured` to the probe's class and disposition in both directions. The reference's rule ("a fixture that disagrees with a probe run is a defect in the fixture") is now enforced on any runner.
./LOGBOOK.md:317:- FINDING: decision 0008 section 4 requires every local driver to bind exactly one closed project-metadata file in the build root, as `go-v1` binds `go.mod`. Kotlin/Native has no candidate. `kotlinc-native` consumes `.kt` sources and an output path and defines no manifest; the ecosystem's manifest is a Gradle script.
./LOGBOOK.md:335:- FINDING: the same defect was present in three more places nobody had challenged — `invalid toolchain "v" in <anything>` (lead), `invalid go version 'v':` (substring anywhere in output), and the TooNew lead-and-tail matcher. Cycle 6 had closed the *fall-through*, not the *matching*.
./LOGBOOK.md:375:- FIX: `go version` replaces `go build ./...` for that position (whole failure surface is `Select`, so exit 0 measures acceptance rather than absence of unrelated failure); `cmdUnknown` added as a fourth state that is not a verdict and always fails; recognition is per-diagnostic-line and must echo the value under test in upstream's own `%q`. The `go`-directive classifier closed too — its fall-through named *rejection*, which launders just as effectively for values the isolated measurement already rejects.
./LOGBOOK.md:401:- FINDING: upstream keeps the two apart and orders them — `cmd/go/internal/modload/modfile.go` raises `*gover.TooNewError` only *after* `modfile.Parse` returns and `gover.Compare(f.Go.Version, gover.Local()) > 0`. Representability is a property of the value; buildability is a property of the host. `$GOROOT/src/internal/gover/gover.go` imports only `cmp` and never consults the local version.
./LOGBOOK.md:402:- FIX: the probe now lifts `internal/gover/gover.go` whole and `FromToolchain` verbatim out of the *probed* toolchain's own `GOROOT`, builds them with that toolchain, and evaluates representability directly; the real command is retained only as corroboration with its outcome classified into accepted / too-new / rejected. The two must agree wherever the shape layer accepts the value.
./LOGBOOK.md:568:- FINDING: `modfile.GoVersionRE` governs the `go` directive and requires a nonzero major plus a **required** minor (`go 1`, `go 0`, `go 1.023`, `go 1.23rc` all rejected). `modfile.ToolchainRE` (`^default$|^go1($|\.)`) governs the `toolchain` value shape, and the version part it yields is validated by `cmd/go/internal/gover.Parse`, which *accepts* a bare major (`go1` → `1.0.0`) and *rejects* a prerelease after an explicit patch (`1.21.3rc1`). Verified on host against `golang.org/x/mod@v0.38.0/modfile/rule.go:329,338` and `GOROOT/src/cmd/go/internal/gover/{toolchain,gover}.go`, not from memory.
./LOGBOOK.md:624:- NOTE: host `win` has no Go toolchain and no `git`. Test binaries must be cross-compiled on macOS; `internal/managerlock` subprocess tests then fail with `cannot run executable found relative to current directory`, and `TestCLIEndToEndInstallStatusAndTamperCheck` fails for the missing `git`. Both are harness artifacts.
./LOGBOOK.md:660:- DECISION: a custom Go toolchain name (`go1.23.4-bigcorp`) is `package_influence_forbidden`, not a metadata mismatch. A mismatch asserts "this claim is false about the resolved toolchain", which invites a later implementation to verify the claim by consulting the named distribution — reopening the selection path the contract exists to close. `toolchain default` is the opposite case: permitted and never honored, because it asserts exactly Curator's own unconditional behavior.
./LOGBOOK.md:670:- DECISION: `manager-worker-v1` stays frozen and Go-only. Decision 0006 binds it to `<GOROOT>/bin/go`, to `<GOROOT>/pkg/tool/...`, and to exactly one `go list` plus one `go build`; re-reading it generically for Rust/Swift/Kotlin would change what a frozen rc.4/rc.5 label means after the fact. `TASK-260728-2spy93` mints `manager-worker-v2` for the six reserved drivers only.
./LOGBOOK.md:671:- FINDING: the previous draft's rejection of `manager-worker-v2` ("it would change every `go-v1` cache key and invalidate rc.5") was false as stated. That cost belongs to *migrating Go* to v2, not to minting v2 for new drivers. Minting for new drivers alone moves no Go byte — verified: rc.5 manifest digest `sha256:9ba9b8ec...` unchanged, two clean regenerations byte-identical, `make release-check VERSION=1.0.0-rc.5` exit 0.
./LOGBOOK.md:677:- FINDING: decision 0007 §2.2 requires `toolchain` REQUIRED on both schema-8 manifest commands and OPTIONAL on the descriptor schema-2 target, while decision 0008 said the local command is exactly three members and the descriptor adds none — `TASK-260728-2jaw7h` could not have satisfied both. Reconciled by naming the three reserved definitions and fixing their exact shapes. The REQUIRED fourth member causes no rebuild: the requirement is a gate, not a build input, so a `go-v1` command moving from schema 7 to 8 produces a byte-identical canonical build input.
./LOGBOOK.md:697:- DECISION: keep `manager-worker-v1`; do not mint `manager-worker-v2`. The two lower graph nodes were always per-operation values bound by the toolchain identity inside the build input, never constants of the policy, so restating them generically changes no `go-v1` byte. Minting a new identity would change every `go-v1` cache key and invalidate the frozen rc.5 candidate for zero security gain. Verified: regeneration byte-identical, pin still `sha256:9ba9b8ec…`.
./LOGBOOK.md:723:- FINDING: the ordering fix costs nothing for Go. Stage A reuses the three package-independent bootstrap probes `protocol/core.md` §4.2 already runs from the manager parent (`go telemetry off`, `go version`, `go env -json …`), and `go version` stdout is already the `V` record of `curator-go-toolchain-v1`. The five Go argument-vector forms stay five; `manager-worker-v1` is untouched. Only the phase changes.
./LOGBOOK.md:738:- FINDING: the descriptor path is part of the external build input, so the rename is a real cache-identity revision, not cosmetic. External `go-repository-v1` cache key `07dd911a…` → `4abc903b…`, external receipt hash `11d2bf4d…` → `0f8f910a…`. Local `go-v1` carries no descriptor and is byte-identical (`1a887eb6…`), which is what scopes the change to the external driver.
./LOGBOOK.md:899:### 0912 — Portable go-v1 control installation moved to the manager parent
./LOGBOOK.md:911:### 0740 — Curator implements portable manager-worker-v1 go-v1
./LOGBOOK.md:918:- DECISION: worker tests drive a real subprocess. `TestMain` gives the test binary the same hidden mode the manager has, and `testdata/stubgo` is a real compiled launcher inside a fingerprintable fake `GOROOT`. Consequence: measured `internal/godriver` coverage reads 72.1 % because worker-side statements execute out of process and are not attributed to the parent profile.
./LOGBOOK.md:944:### 0506 — go-v1 execution policy: portable manager-worker-v1, hardened deferred
./LOGBOOK.md:945:- DECISION: curator-spec rc.5 `go-v1`/`go-repository-v1` now declare a named execution policy, `manager-worker-v1`, implemented on macOS and Windows. Replaces the earlier `TASK-260720-1zntv0` proposal of hardened-Linux-only real builds with `host_profile_unsupported` everywhere else.
./LOGBOOK.md:949:- FINDING: binding `execution_policy` into `goBuildPolicyV1` necessarily changes every `go-v1` logical cache key, so the rc.4 receipt example `93217cf1...` could not stay byte-frozen. Handled by moving it out of `TestRC4CompiledArtifactsRemainByteFrozen` into a dedicated non-alias guard that pins the old digest as negative evidence.
